  "text": "She laughed silently, inside her own head, behind a tent flap. And got caught. Genesis eighteen. Three visitors come to Abraham's tent in the heat of the day. And one of them says, \"Lo, Sarah thy wife shall have a son.\" And Sarah is listening at the tent door behind him. The text is blunt. They are old. It says plainly it had ceased to be with Sarah after the manner of women. So she laughed within herself. Not out loud. Inside. And said, \"After I am waxed old shall I have pleasure, my lord being old also?\" And then the Lord says to Abraham, \"Wherefore did Sarah laugh?\" Caught. Instantly. Laughing silently, inside her own head, behind a tent flap. And then, \"Is any thing too hard for the Lord?\" And Sarah, understandably, panics. \"I laughed not.\" Because she was afraid. And he says, gently and immovably, \"Nay; but thou didst laugh.\" He does not withdraw the promise. Genesis twenty-one. She has the boy. Names him Isaac. Which means laughter. And says, \"God hath made me to laugh.\"",
